finding: Stage 5 is blocked by two independently reproduced input defects in examples/interact/src/interact.ts (SampleInteraction / UseInteraction), despite all 31 existing cheap tests and all four browser tests passing. 1. Lost focus before a pending hold's first consuming tick does not cancel that hold. Reproduction using the existing scene(Action.Hold) fixture: pressKey(w,"KeyE"); w.step(0); move the camera's Transform.translation.x to 5; w.step(0); move x back to 0; w.step(0); step(60). Expected active==0 and a fresh press required; actual active==1. SampleInteraction latches cancelled, but UseInteraction subsequently treats the still-pending original press as fresh and overwrites spent=1 with spent=0. The existing lost-focus test starts with 30 already-consumed ticks, so cannot expose this. Smallest fix: invalidate pending hold-start eligibility when focus/release cancellation occurs AFTER its sampled press, without discarding a genuinely fresh press sampled after cancellation; add this zero-tick departure/return regression. 2. Multiple sampled taps before a consuming tick collapse into one action. On scene(Action.Lamp): press E; step(0); release E; step(0); press E; step(0); release E; step(0); step(). Expected Usable.presses==2 and active==0; actual presses==1 and active==1. UseInteraction reduces the accumulated press counter to a boolean and then sets lastPresses to the latest value. Smallest fix: preserve/consume pending instantaneous presses with their sampled entity (at least the same-target count delta), retaining the no-target-transfer guarantee; add a two-tap zero-tick regression and multiple-target cancellation coverage. This is distinct from any device-level Set coalescing: these are two separately sampled presses. Independently examined: full unit and project instructions, worker reports, interaction and room source, cheap tests, stepped browser fixture and rendering assertions, Text sorting and billboard shader formulas, owner test, and eight supplied captures. Re-ran cheap suite (31 pass), Text owner suite (2 pass), and browser suite (4 pass, real M4 Max). Added temporary adversarial tests: the two cases above fail, a 4-tick hold-completion frame passes. All temporary source changes were restored; no source diff or commit remains. Mutation evidence: removing pressedTarget matching makes all nine queued transfer tests fail; disabling button return makes its regression fail; restoring the old held-block movement makes its regression fail; sharing label/key depth makes the angle-depth test fail; restoring the detached/small ring makes its layout test fail; reverting Text/index.ts to before 430d6fd1 makes the Screen owner regression fail. Thus the reported target-transfer and Screen sorting causes are real, but not a complete input robustness proof. Notes, not additional blockers: the Text formulas agree with billboardCorner for Screen, YLocked and World (YLocked horizontal offset has zero view-depth contribution; vertical centre uses column length); the new owner regression proves Screen, not YLocked transparent sorting. Public imports and entity-keyed plain state are appropriate; no demonstrated residual cross-target activation. Inspected captures show the enclosed room, seated button, legible prompt and lamp light escaping the shade, consistent with the main visual claims. hold-view-1.8.png still has the ring's top crossing the block outline, contrary to the report's stronger outline-clearance wording, but not a disappearing/z-fighting prompt part. No live physical-monitor play or exhaustive fast-turn/near-plane sweep was performed, and Linux evidence was read from the worker report rather than independently dispatched.
